The matching has four rounds in all, and the rounds of one nub ride the beacon

The video of run 581 says what the board is doing.  The heartbeat went from
V1938 to V20315 in nine seconds, and the same two lines came back in the
meantime: the NioUfs probe with its provider and its compatible string,
and the AppleARMCPU candidate done.  The matching was running, round after
round, over the same candidates.

It had no end.  keepGuessing stays true while the service keeps asking to be
configured, so the nub is matched again and again -- and the boot thread,
which runs the matching now, never comes out of it.  That is why the
waypoint stayed at 6 and bsd_init never came.

So the matching has four rounds.  Four rounds in all, and not four for each
nub in it.  That distinction is the whole of this patch, and it cost twenty
rounds to find.

The bound that run 583 added was per nub: every nub the platform expert
publishes got four rounds of its own, each round probing every candidate
again, and every candidate's start() waiting two seconds for a resource
that is never going to come (978387b gave that wait an end).  So the time
the matching took was the number of nubs in the tree times four rounds
times nine candidates times two seconds.  The machine does not have that
long: it is taken away about a hundred seconds into every round by the
hardware watchdog, which the board's own note about the ten second
watchdog records, and the measurement of the phone's window says 104
seconds from the kernel's first mark to the reset.  So the matching never
finished, the boot thread never left it, and every photograph since has
shown the same first round -- the panel's last line is D5, the mark every
candidate that reached the loop leaves behind, and the line numbers run in
one direction without a break.

Counted over the whole tree, four rounds is what the matching needs.  The
first round probes the candidates and starts the driver, and the nubs the
platform expert publishes are matched in that same round, because the count
is shared.  After the fourth round the boot thread leaves the matching, the
waypoint moves on past the lockdown, and the panel finally shows what is
behind the matching.

The count is bumped where the rounds are counted and not in
probeCandidates(), because that is called only when the candidate list is
not empty: a nub with no candidates still costs a round, and a bound
counted there would not count it.

Two round counters ride the beacon now, because they are not the same
number.  The letter q is the rounds of the whole matching, and the letter u
is the rounds of the nub being matched, which is what the bound applies to.
Before this the letter q was bumped in probeCandidates(), so it counted the
times the candidate loop was entered over the whole tree; the board read it
as the rounds of one nub and waited for it to stop near four, and it could
not have stopped there whatever the bound did.  A photograph now says which
of the two it is looking at.

